23:30:53 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Thank you for saying that plainly. It’s not just a worry or a one-off pattern you’re noticing; it’s a repeated, exhausting reality. When you’ve been in enough rooms where this happens every single time, “how to establish myself” starts to feel like a bad joke—because the problem isn’t your establishment, it’s a dynamic that seems baked into the room itself. I hear you, and I’m not going to pretend it’s all in your head or that the right opening line will fix it. So instead of advice that assumes the best, let’s talk about what works when you’re walking into yet another room where you *know* it’s going to happen. The goal shifts from “changing the culture” (exhausting, unfair burden on you) to “preserving your energy, making your impact, and not letting them drive you up the wall.” Here’s how to operate in that reality: **1. Assume the pattern, and stop being surprised by it.** That sounds bleak, but it’s actually freeing. You don’t have to brace for impact every time—you can just budget for it. Think of it like traffic: you don’t get emotionally hijacked by every red light; you just build in extra time. Before the meeting, remind yourself: “I will be interrupted. I will be talked over. That’s their noise, not my emergency.” This mental shift lowers the adrenaline spike so you can stay strategic instead of reactive. **2. Use the “broken record with a ratchet” technique.** When you’re constantly interrupted, you don’t need a new, clever rejoinder each time. You need one or two phrases you can repeat calmly, without venom. For example: - “I’m going to finish this point.” - “I’m not done.” - “Let me land this.” Say it with the same tone you’d use to say “pass the salt.” If they keep interrupting, you keep saying it, and you do not yield the floor until you’ve made your point. The ratchet is that you can escalate *very slightly* only if needed—maybe a small hand gesture like a “stop” sign or a brief, direct eye contact. But the power is in the repetition, not the escalation. **3. Stop filling silences and doing emotional labor.** In these rooms, women often end up smoothing things over, drawing out quieter voices, or managing the tension. Don’t. Let awkward silences sit. Let the interrupter’s words hang there. You don’t have to rescue the conversation or make everyone comfortable. Your only job is to get your work done. If a man talks over you and the room lets it happen, you can simply pause, look at your notes, and wait. Then, when he’s done, you can say, “As I was saying…” without any anger. You’re not ignoring what happened; you’re choosing not to absorb it. **4. Document your contributions in real time—for yourself.** This isn’t about “gotcha,” it’s about sanity. Keep a small notebook or a private doc where you jot down a few key points you made, especially ones that were ignored or co-opted. At the end of the meeting, you can send a brief follow-up email if needed: “Just to recap what I recommended on the budget item…” This creates a paper trail without you having to battle in the moment, and it reminds *you* that you are making substantive contributions, even if the room doesn’t give you credit. **5. Pick your battles mercilessly.** In a room where interruptions are constant, don’t fight for every sentence. It will grind you down. Save your “I’m not done” for the moments where the point is critical, where your expertise is being overridden, or where a decision could go badly without your input. For the rest, let it go—not out of weakness, but out of strategic triage. Think of your energy like a battery; plug it only into the outlets that matter. **6. Find your exit or your ally, even if it’s outside the room.** If you’re completely isolated inside that committee, build a relationship with someone outside it who gets it—a trusted colleague, a mentor, a friend in another department. Vent, strategize, and afterwards, get a sanity check. Sometimes just saying “It happened again” to someone who believes you is enough to refill your tank. If there’s even one man in the room who seems uncomfortable with the dynamic, you can test an alliance: a quiet “I noticed you didn’t get to finish your point about X either” in a side conversation can sometimes crack open a door. Most of all, I want to affirm: this is not a you problem. It’s not about your tone, your confidence, or your comebacks. It’s a systemic, deeply ingrained pattern that you’ve been forced to navigate over and over. You are allowed to be tired of it. You’re allowed to protect your peace. Does it help to have these more “this-is-the-grind” strategies, or are you feeling like what you really needed today was just to be heard and have someone say, “Yeah, it sucks”? I’m here for either one.
